## Impact Details

**Org-dependency verification:** Querying the org's metadata index for `SLO_Sellout_Report__c`, `SLO_Sellout_Item__c`, `Invoice__c`, `Invoice_Line_Item__c`, `SLO_SelloutImportController`, `SLO_SelloutAccountMatcher`, and all other apexClass/custom-object IDs listed in the architecture document returned **zero rows** across multiple query shapes (by name, by label, by metadata ID, and by wildcard pattern on `%sellout%`/`%SLO%`/`%invoice%`). The org's actual custom-object inventory (5 `__c` objects: `RFC_Forecast__c`, `WRM_ActionPlan__c`, `WRM_KeyReviewInsights__c`, `WRM_RollingForecast__c`, `WRM_SalesAnalytics__c`) and 19 Apex classes (all `RFC_`, `WRM_`, `TRBL__`, `DistributorPortalDashboardCtrl`, `SalesVisitSummaryCtrl` prefixed) confirm these Sellout/Invoice components are **not yet present** in this org's indexed metadata. This is consistent with the "No affected items found" / "No deactivation candidates found" results supplied for this analysis.

Because the dependency graph has no record of these components, I cannot verify runtime interaction with pre-existing automation from the org side — findings below are based on the code/architecture descriptions provided plus confirmation that no conflicting Account/Invoice/SLO automation exists in this org today.

- **SLO_SelloutImportController** (Apex Class) — Risk: **Low**. New methods are additive (`getFilteredSelloutItems`, `getItemStatusCounts`); the existing `getSelloutItems` is explicitly preserved for backward compatibility per build instructions, and no other org component was found referencing this class, so no breaking signature changes propagate. Recommended: confirm via unit tests that `applySellout`/`startAsyncApplySellout` correctly treat `Draft` as a valid starting status, since this is a behavior change to a status-gating condition that the LWC also depends on.

- **SLO_SelloutValidationTest** (Apex Class, test) — Risk: **Low**. Pure test class; does not execute in production transactions. Primary risk is code coverage/test-pass risk at deploy time rather than runtime impact. Recommended: run full test suite including `AC_SLOSelloutItemImportBatch` and `AC_SLOSelloutApplyBatch` chains before deployment, since this test validates cross-batch chaining behavior (import → match → draft status) that is easy to break with partial deployments.

- **SLO_Automatic_Sellout__c (Account checkbox)** — Risk: **Low**. Not found referenced by any existing Account trigger, flow, or validation rule in this org. Its only consumer per the architecture doc is the new `SLO_SelloutInvoiceReviewScheduler`/`SLO_SelloutInvoiceReviewBatch`, which are being introduced by this same project. No pre-existing automation reads or writes this field, so no collision risk exists in this org currently.

- **SLO_Sellout_Report__c.SLO_Status__c (picklist, adds "Draft")** — Risk: **Low**. Restricted picklist value addition is additive and non-breaking for existing records (none exist yet, since the object isn't in this org). The only consumers are the project's own Apex classes and LWC, which are being updated in the same deployment to recognize `Draft`. Recommended: deploy this field change in the same release as `AC_SLOSelloutItemImportBatch`, `AC_SLOSelloutApplyBatch`, and `LWC_SelloutImportManager2` — deploying the picklist value without the corresponding Apex/LWC updates (or vice versa) would leave the status-gating logic ("Apply Sellout enabled when Draft or Completed") out of sync, causing the UI button to stay disabled or batches to reject the new status as invalid.

- **SLO_Sellout_Item__c.SLO_Processing_Status__c (picklist, adds "Pending_Decision")** — Risk: **Low**. Same reasoning as above: additive restricted value, consumed only by this project's own controller/batch/LWC updates being deployed together. Recommended: same co-deployment caveat — `SLO_SelloutApplyBatch`'s match mode and the LWC's bulk-decision UI (Requirement 8) must ship together with this value, or items could get stuck in a status the UI doesn't yet know how to display/act on.

**Internal (cross-component) consistency risks worth flagging, independent of the org's existing automation:**

- **Status-gating consistency across layers (Medium/High within the feature, not org-wide):** The `Draft` and `Pending_Decision` picklist additions are load-bearing for three independent artifacts that must all agree: the picklist metadata itself, `SLO_SelloutImportController`/`SLO_SelloutApplyBatch` Apex logic, and `LWC_SelloutImportManager2`'s `canApplySellout` getter. If any one of these three ships without the others in the same deployment window, users could see the Apply Sellout button incorrectly enabled/disabled, or batches could throw `System.DmlException: INVALID_OR_NULL_FOR_RESTRICTED_PICKLIST` if Apex attempts to set a status value not yet deployed. Recommend deploying all Sellout LWC project components as a single atomic release/validation deployment rather than piecemeal.

- **Batch chaining governor-limit exposure (Medium, self-contained to the feature):** `AC_SLOSelloutItemImportBatch` now chains into `AC_SLOSelloutApplyBatch` (match mode) automatically on finish, which itself may chain into invoice-mode processing. Each batch execution context resets governor limits, so this is generally safe, but the chained batches plus the synchronous `getFilteredSelloutItems`/`getItemStatusCounts` SOQL (GROUP BY aggregate query) run per Draft-review page load. For accounts with very large sellout item volumes, repeated status-count aggregation queries during UI polling could add up; recommend confirming these controller methods are not polled too frequently from the LWC.

## Components To Deactivate

**None.** No existing org automation (triggers, flows, or batch/scheduled Apex) was found in this org's metadata that fires on `Account`, `Invoice__c`, `SLO_Sellout_Report__c`, or `SLO_Sellout_Item__c` with overlapping purpose. The org's single active Apex trigger, `WRM_RollingForecastTrigger`, fires exclusively on `WRM_RollingForecast__c` and has no relationship to this project. The supplied "Existing Org Automation That May Need Deactivation" input also confirmed no candidates. No deactivation steps are required for this deployment.

**Note on verification scope:** Because the Sellout/Invoice custom objects and related Apex classes are not present in this org's currently indexed metadata, this analysis could not directly inspect their live field-level security, record-triggered flow bindings, or page layout placements in this specific org. If this org is in fact the intended deployment target and these components are expected to already exist (e.g., due to indexing lag or a metadata sync gap), re-run metadata indexing before deployment and repeat this analysis — the "Low risk" conclusions above are contingent on the confirmed absence of conflicting automation, not on confirmed presence of the base Sellout package itself.
